{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"length","index":0,"message":{"content":null,"reasoning":"We need answer in German, max ~700 words. Need act as Ratssitz in Hause omegaflow. Must not invent facts. Use only given evidence. We can reason. Need for each question: verdict A/B/C/D/pending, 2-3 sentences reasoning, strongest rift/counterexample, if uncertainty explicitly pending + what to measure.\n\nNeed be careful: A=A; \"0 honored: null-echt | absent | riss; keine harten Schwellen; PII bleibt im Haus\". So no hard thresholds. We can give nuanced. Need maybe indicate pending for some. Let's analyze each.\n\nF1: Where is receipt in token ledger? Today state only in sender ledger. Receiver can't ack own ingress: `xfer close` on foreign token hard error. Receipt and self-completion both CLOSED indistinguishable. `register_lookup --addressed <linie>` lists closed blocks further; `--fired` remains 0, completed reads as open. Early `xfer ack` arm retracted (commit eb2f1b749, -202 lines). Options:\nA — own act `ACK <tok> by=<linie>.<sitz>` in receiver's ledger; ACKED distinguishable from CLOSED.\nB — as today: receiver writes CLOSED into emitter ledger.\nC — no new act: receipt state derived read-only from token ledger.\nD — one ledger per token instead of per line (both write same store).\nSecond question `--addressed`: S1 hide closed blocks · S2 continue list, but with state column.\n\nWe need verdict. Likely A + S2? Let's reason. Evidence: receiver cannot quit own ingress; receipt and self-completion both CLOSED; register_lookup lists closed blocks; --fired 0, completed reads as open. Need distinguish ACKED from CLOSED. A introduces own act in receiver ledger, preserving per-line ledger? It distinguishes. B is status quo problematic. C derivation read-only: if ledger lacks ack info, can't derive. Unless token ledger has something? Given today only emitter ledger, receiver can't write. So C impossible without new data. D one ledger per token: architectural bigger, but could solve? However \"Ein Ledger je Token statt je Linie (beide schreiben dieselbe Ablage)\" might violate per-line? No evidence. A seems minimal and consistent with per-line ledgers. But early `xfer ack` arm retracted; maybe suggests ack was tried and removed. Could be pending if need know why retracted. We have commit eb2f1b749, -202 lines. That's evidence of prior attempt. Strongest rift: retraction indicates A may have failed (complexity? conflicts?); without reason, pending? But question asks Verdikt. We can say A with S2, but perhaps pending on why ack retracted. The instruction: \"bei Unsicherheit ausdrücklich pending\". We can include pending if unsure. But format allows verdict A + note pending? It says \"Verdikt: <A|B|C|D|pending>\". Could say \"A, pending auf Rücknahme-Grund\"? Maybe not. We need choose. Let's see: A is clearly best to distinguish ACKED vs CLOSED. But \"ein früher gebauter `xfer ack`-Arm wurde zurückgenommen\" is a counterevidence: maybe A was tried and failed. But no reason. The strongest rift is exactly that. If no reason, maybe pending? But we can still recommend A if reason not shown. The question: \"Wo wird die Quittung im Token-Ledger belegt?\" The evidence says receiver cannot quit own ingress. A gives own act in receiver's ledger. That's likely correct. Second: `--addressed` closed blocks continue listed; `--fired` 0, completed reads open. S2 is better: continue list with state column, because S1 hiding could hide history and not fix --fired? Actually if closed blocks are hidden, Erledigtes would not read as open? But `--fired` remains 0? Hmm. The problem: closed blocks listed further; --fired remains 0, completed reads as open. If S1 hides closed blocks, then completed blocks disappear, so not read as open? But maybe then they are absent rather than open? The phrase: \"S1 geschlossene Blöcke ausblenden · S2 weiter listen, aber mit Zustandsspalte.\" S2 explicitly makes state visible, distinguishing. S1 might hide legitimate history and not provide state. Given \"0 honored: null-echt | absent | riss\" maybe absent is not honored? So S2.\n\nF2: R3 from R-2026-10-17-a: Concurrency key of register-coverage. Should `register-coverage.yml` change to `group: register-coverage-${{ github.sha }}` with `cancel-in-progress: true` (like `ci-gate` since 2026-10-08) instead of today `group: …-${{ github.ref }}` with `false`? Measured: push on main fires 5–7 gates; last 100 runs = 25 different HEAD SHAs; in 10 minutes eight register-coverage runs, seven cancelled — in a concurrency group a new run cancels waiting predecessors, `cancel-in-progress: false` only protects running. Gate checks HEAD state of register, no per-SHA artifact. Previous round 3:3 tie. A per-SHA (GLM, DeepSeek, Lumo: \"HEAD is defined per SHA\") · B per-ref (Fugu, Mistral, Kimi: \"address of HEAD is ref, per-SHA addresses past\"). Kimi practical: cancel mid-write can leave inconsistent state; gate must ignore `cancelled`.\nOptions: A per-SHA + true · B per-ref + true (only latest run lives) · C debounce/batch.\n\nNeed verdict. Evidence: Gate checks HEAD state of register, no per-SHA artifact. So per-SHA group seems wrong: each SHA run is a snapshot? But if gate checks HEAD, then only latest HEAD matters. Per-ref + true means only latest run lives, cancelling all older/waiting. But cancel mid-write risk. Per-SHA + true: different SHA groups don't cancel each other? Wait: group per SHA means each commit's runs share group? Actually if group is per SHA, a new run on same SHA cancels previous same SHA. But pushes have different SHAs, so runs for different SHAs are in different groups, so they don't cancel each other. Then you'd have many runs for different SHAs running concurrently, which could be bad. But measured: in 10 minutes eight runs, seven cancelled — that's with per-ref false? Wait today group per-ref false: all runs same ref group, new run queues, `cancel-in-progress: false` protects running but not waiting? Actually GitHub concurrency: if `cancel-in-progress: false`, a new run is queued behind running? Wait semantics: If a concurrency group has a running job and new one arrives, with `cancel-in-progress: false`, the new one waits (pending). If another arrives, does it cancel the pending one? GitHub docs: \"When a concurrent job or workflow is queued, if another job or workflow using the same concurrency group in the repository is in progress, the queued job or workflow will be pending. Any previously pending job or workflow in the concurrency group will be canceled.\" So yes, new pending cancels previously pending, only running protected. So with per-ref false, many pushes cause waiting predecessors cancelled. Seven cancelled. That's bad if every SHA matters. But gate checks HEAD state, no per-SHA artifact. So only latest HEAD matters. Thus B per-ref + true (only latest lives) is appropriate: new run cancels running older, so only latest HEAD run. But risk mid-write inconsistency. Kimi says cancel mid-write can leave inconsistent state; gate must ignore `cancelled`. If gate ignores cancelled, then B works. But if cancellation mid-write corrupts register, need mitigation. Could be pending? We need decide. Given evidence: gate checks HEAD state, no per-SHA artifact. Thus per-ref + true is logically aligned: only latest HEAD state matters. A per-SHA would not cancel across SHAs, so many runs for different HEADs could run, but they all check current HEAD? Actually if a run for old SHA runs, it checks HEAD at runtime, which may be newer. That's waste. B reduces waste. But risk: cancel mid-write. If the gate writes? It's register-coverage, maybe coverage check? \"register-coverage\" maybe reads register and checks coverage. It might write? Kimi says cancel mid-write can leave inconsistent state. If it only reads, no issue. But \"mid-write\" implies it writes. Need measure: does register-coverage write? Not given. So uncertainty. Could say B, with pending on whether it writes; if writes, need C/debounce or ignore cancelled. But format: verdict B? Let's see. The previous round tie 3:3. We can break tie with B, because gate checks HEAD not per-SHA. Riss: mid-write inconsistency; if it writes, B is unsafe. What to measure: whether register-coverage mutates state, and whether cancelled runs can leave partial writes. If yes, C debounce/batch or B with cancellation ignored. The question asks \"Soll ... umgestellt werden?\" Options: A per-SHA + true · B per-ref + true · C Debounce/Batch. I'd say B, pending on write behavior. But maybe C is safer: debounce/batch reduces run count, avoids cancellation. However C is more complex. Evidence: 5-7 gates per push, 8 runs in 10 min, 7 cancelled. A debounce could batch. But B per-ref + true directly addresses \"only latest HEAD lives\" and aligns with HEAD state. The strongest rift: cancel mid-write. If gate ignores cancelled, but mid-write can leave inconsistent state. So B with caveat. We can say \"B, pending auf Schreibverhalten\". The instruction: if uncertainty, explicitly pending + what to measure. So we can give \"B (pending: ob register-coverage","reasoning_content":"We need answer in German, max ~700 words. Need act as Ratssitz in Hause omegaflow. Must not invent facts. Use only given evidence. We can reason. Need for each question: verdict A/B/C/D/pending, 2-3 sentences reasoning, strongest rift/counterexample, if uncertainty explicitly pending + what to measure.\n\nNeed be careful: A=A; \"0 honored: null-echt | absent | riss; keine harten Schwellen; PII bleibt im Haus\". So no hard thresholds. We can give nuanced. Need maybe indicate pending for some. Let's analyze each.\n\nF1: Where is receipt in token ledger? Today state only in sender ledger. Receiver can't ack own ingress: `xfer close` on foreign token hard error. Receipt and self-completion both CLOSED indistinguishable. `register_lookup --addressed <linie>` lists closed blocks further; `--fired` remains 0, completed reads as open. Early `xfer ack` arm retracted (commit eb2f1b749, -202 lines). Options:\nA — own act `ACK <tok> by=<linie>.<sitz>` in receiver's ledger; ACKED distinguishable from CLOSED.\nB — as today: receiver writes CLOSED into emitter ledger.\nC — no new act: receipt state derived read-only from token ledger.\nD — one ledger per token instead of per line (both write same store).\nSecond question `--addressed`: S1 hide closed blocks · S2 continue list, but with state column.\n\nWe need verdict. Likely A + S2? Let's reason. Evidence: receiver cannot quit own ingress; receipt and self-completion both CLOSED; register_lookup lists closed blocks; --fired 0, completed reads as open. Need distinguish ACKED from CLOSED. A introduces own act in receiver ledger, preserving per-line ledger? It distinguishes. B is status quo problematic. C derivation read-only: if ledger lacks ack info, can't derive. Unless token ledger has something? Given today only emitter ledger, receiver can't write. So C impossible without new data. D one ledger per token: architectural bigger, but could solve? However \"Ein Ledger je Token statt je Linie (beide schreiben dieselbe Ablage)\" might violate per-line? No evidence. A seems minimal and consistent with per-line ledgers. But early `xfer ack` arm retracted; maybe suggests ack was tried and removed. Could be pending if need know why retracted. We have commit eb2f1b749, -202 lines. That's evidence of prior attempt. Strongest rift: retraction indicates A may have failed (complexity? conflicts?); without reason, pending? But question asks Verdikt. We can say A with S2, but perhaps pending on why ack retracted. The instruction: \"bei Unsicherheit ausdrücklich pending\". We can include pending if unsure. But format allows verdict A + note pending? It says \"Verdikt: <A|B|C|D|pending>\". Could say \"A, pending auf Rücknahme-Grund\"? Maybe not. We need choose. Let's see: A is clearly best to distinguish ACKED vs CLOSED. But \"ein früher gebauter `xfer ack`-Arm wurde zurückgenommen\" is a counterevidence: maybe A was tried and failed. But no reason. The strongest rift is exactly that. If no reason, maybe pending? But we can still recommend A if reason not shown. The question: \"Wo wird die Quittung im Token-Ledger belegt?\" The evidence says receiver cannot quit own ingress. A gives own act in receiver's ledger. That's likely correct. Second: `--addressed` closed blocks continue listed; `--fired` 0, completed reads open. S2 is better: continue list with state column, because S1 hiding could hide history and not fix --fired? Actually if closed blocks are hidden, Erledigtes would not read as open? But `--fired` remains 0? Hmm. The problem: closed blocks listed further; --fired remains 0, completed reads as open. If S1 hides closed blocks, then completed blocks disappear, so not read as open? But maybe then they are absent rather than open? The phrase: \"S1 geschlossene Blöcke ausblenden · S2 weiter listen, aber mit Zustandsspalte.\" S2 explicitly makes state visible, distinguishing. S1 might hide legitimate history and not provide state. Given \"0 honored: null-echt | absent | riss\" maybe absent is not honored? So S2.\n\nF2: R3 from R-2026-10-17-a: Concurrency key of register-coverage. Should `register-coverage.yml` change to `group: register-coverage-${{ github.sha }}` with `cancel-in-progress: true` (like `ci-gate` since 2026-10-08) instead of today `group: …-${{ github.ref }}` with `false`? Measured: push on main fires 5–7 gates; last 100 runs = 25 different HEAD SHAs; in 10 minutes eight register-coverage runs, seven cancelled — in a concurrency group a new run cancels waiting predecessors, `cancel-in-progress: false` only protects running. Gate checks HEAD state of register, no per-SHA artifact. Previous round 3:3 tie. A per-SHA (GLM, DeepSeek, Lumo: \"HEAD is defined per SHA\") · B per-ref (Fugu, Mistral, Kimi: \"address of HEAD is ref, per-SHA addresses past\"). Kimi practical: cancel mid-write can leave inconsistent state; gate must ignore `cancelled`.\nOptions: A per-SHA + true · B per-ref + true (only latest run lives) · C debounce/batch.\n\nNeed verdict. Evidence: Gate checks HEAD state of register, no per-SHA artifact. So per-SHA group seems wrong: each SHA run is a snapshot? But if gate checks HEAD, then only latest HEAD matters. Per-ref + true means only latest run lives, cancelling all older/waiting. But cancel mid-write risk. Per-SHA + true: different SHA groups don't cancel each other? Wait: group per SHA means each commit's runs share group? Actually if group is per SHA, a new run on same SHA cancels previous same SHA. But pushes have different SHAs, so runs for different SHAs are in different groups, so they don't cancel each other. Then you'd have many runs for different SHAs running concurrently, which could be bad. But measured: in 10 minutes eight runs, seven cancelled — that's with per-ref false? Wait today group per-ref false: all runs same ref group, new run queues, `cancel-in-progress: false` protects running but not waiting? Actually GitHub concurrency: if `cancel-in-progress: false`, a new run is queued behind running? Wait semantics: If a concurrency group has a running job and new one arrives, with `cancel-in-progress: false`, the new one waits (pending). If another arrives, does it cancel the pending one? GitHub docs: \"When a concurrent job or workflow is queued, if another job or workflow using the same concurrency group in the repository is in progress, the queued job or workflow will be pending. Any previously pending job or workflow in the concurrency group will be canceled.\" So yes, new pending cancels previously pending, only running protected. So with per-ref false, many pushes cause waiting predecessors cancelled. Seven cancelled. That's bad if every SHA matters. But gate checks HEAD state, no per-SHA artifact. So only latest HEAD matters. Thus B per-ref + true (only latest lives) is appropriate: new run cancels running older, so only latest HEAD run. But risk mid-write inconsistency. Kimi says cancel mid-write can leave inconsistent state; gate must ignore `cancelled`. If gate ignores cancelled, then B works. But if cancellation mid-write corrupts register, need mitigation. Could be pending? We need decide. Given evidence: gate checks HEAD state, no per-SHA artifact. Thus per-ref + true is logically aligned: only latest HEAD state matters. A per-SHA would not cancel across SHAs, so many runs for different HEADs could run, but they all check current HEAD? Actually if a run for old SHA runs, it checks HEAD at runtime, which may be newer. That's waste. B reduces waste. But risk: cancel mid-write. If the gate writes? It's register-coverage, maybe coverage check? \"register-coverage\" maybe reads register and checks coverage. It might write? Kimi says cancel mid-write can leave inconsistent state. If it only reads, no issue. But \"mid-write\" implies it writes. Need measure: does register-coverage write? Not given. So uncertainty. Could say B, with pending on whether it writes; if writes, need C/debounce or ignore cancelled. But format: verdict B? Let's see. The previous round tie 3:3. We can break tie with B, because gate checks HEAD not per-SHA. Riss: mid-write inconsistency; if it writes, B is unsafe. What to measure: whether register-coverage mutates state, and whether cancelled runs can leave partial writes. If yes, C debounce/batch or B with cancellation ignored. The question asks \"Soll ... umgestellt werden?\" Options: A per-SHA + true · B per-ref + true · C Debounce/Batch. I'd say B, pending on write behavior. But maybe C is safer: debounce/batch reduces run count, avoids cancellation. However C is more complex. Evidence: 5-7 gates per push, 8 runs in 10 min, 7 cancelled. A debounce could batch. But B per-ref + true directly addresses \"only latest HEAD lives\" and aligns with HEAD state. The strongest rift: cancel mid-write. If gate ignores cancelled, but mid-write can leave inconsistent state. So B with caveat. We can say \"B, pending au